#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055648} :activity/id #uuid "a47ac5b8-841f-560b-8cb9-a5aa92267125" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050041 :topic/id #uuid "28124701-15c1-562d-b441-8c061aed5e28" :topic/math-academy-id 36} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055932 :tutorial/content "Suppose the Taylor (Maclaurin) series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{2n+1} x^{2n+1}\n$$\n\nand converges to $f(x)$ for $|x| < R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. How can we calculate the first three non-zero terms of Taylor series for the derivative, $f'(x),$ about $x=0?$\n\nAll we need to do is differentiate the Taylor series of $f(x).$ Writing out the first three terms, we get\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{2n+1} x^{2n+1} = x - \\dfrac{x^3}{3} + \\dfrac{x^5}{5} - \\cdots.\n$$\n\nNow, to find $f'(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})-⋯ \\\\  & =1-{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! The radius of convergence of the Taylor series for $f'(x)$ is $R,$ the same as $f(x).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "59207cd1-3d1f-52ac-9acb-354bc8dff029" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1318 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0d27720d-4ea4-522a-9aab-b1b543167de5" :step/math-academy-id 2591 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055649}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055649 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054682 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054581 :question/id #uuid "3c7dd3ad-eaa9-5312-adaf-3a0772e845f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-762"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67e6a951-2645-5440-8059-14f0182933ff" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932}]